  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a ridge-waveguide semiconductor laser integrated on a silicon platform. It shows a long device with repeated metal contacts on top, while a cutaway view reveals the layered internal structure and where light is confined.\n\nAt the core is a small active region labeled as a quantum well inside III–V semiconductor layers, including p-GaAs above and i-GaAs/n-GaAs below, with InGaP at the sides. A blue oval marks the optical mode, indicating that the generated light is concentrated around the quantum well within the ridge. Current appears to be injected vertically from the metal contacts through the upper layers into the active region, enabling lasing.\n\nThe laser sits above a silicon substrate, separated and laterally shaped by silicon dioxide. A narrow trench under the ridge seems to help define or isolate the optical and electrical structure. Overall, the image emphasizes how a compound-semiconductor laser stack can be built directly on silicon, combining standard silicon support materials with a III–V light-emitting region to create an electrically pumped on-chip laser.",
